#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069547} :activity/id #uuid "7b10485d-7fa4-5254-83ce-37fba428298f" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050089 :topic/id #uuid "2cb07e2e-6475-548a-8275-28e63ed7fbf9" :topic/math-academy-id 460} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069547 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069956 :tutorial/content "The **circumference** of a circle is the length of the path that goes around the circle.\n\n![](images/ac/ac8f31a99961c51cb53695d9daab017126d1316b720e6383b876824d7c7947bd.png)\n\nThe circumference is usually denoted by the letter $C$.\n\nOne important fact about the circumference is that, for *any* circle, no matter how small or large it is, the ratio of circumference $C$ to diameter $d$ is a fixed number! Specifically,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{C}{d} = {\\color{blue}\\boldsymbol{\\pi}} \\approx 3.141\\,592\\,654..\n$$\n\nThis important irrational number is called **pi** (pronounced \"pie\") and we use the Greek letter $\\pi$ to express it. So, we can calculate the circumference by using the following formula:\n\n$$\nC = πd\n$$\n\nHere, our circle has diameter $4\\,\\textrm{cm}$, so its circumference is\n\n$$\nC = \\pi \\cdot 4 = 4\\pi \\approx 12.566\\,\\mathrm{cm}\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal digits.\n\nSince the diameter is twice as long as the radius ($d=2r$), we can also write the formula for the circumference as\n\n$$\nC = \\pi d = 2\\pi r\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "e75a0c81-7a99-51d8-85ff-de6951125d7b" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2423 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "3437ac3b-f6ef-5e78-8b46-bca7d485abd4" :step/math-academy-id 10876 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069548}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069548 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068681 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068542 :question/id #uuid "ba3d3abd-4b93-5705-8fcb-cb88b58dad2f" :question/math-academy-id "e-3178"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "62bb3b79-407f-511b-913d-f47b3ea6d822" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048438} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049856}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069254} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069255}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Circumference of a Circle Given the Diameter"} :step/id #uuid "51b49060-4f8e-51b6-a0fb-696a9394fe72" :step/math-academy-id 10877 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069549}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069549 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068682 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068543 :question/id #uuid "d26dfc25-605c-5d73-80bc-15801b20beb5" :question/math-academy-id "e-3179"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "27538979-a350-50d2-9c55-cebf0bd7e78b" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069256} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069257}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Circumference of a Circle Given the Radius"} :step/id #uuid "0f70e49e-370a-5a2c-9320-28d0225c9e1e" :step/math-academy-id 10878 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069550}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069550 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068683 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068544 :question/id #uuid "af77eddf-8816-5b03-a53d-0f29f1f1a1b3" :question/math-academy-id "e-3180"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "09b24ba2-e9c9-5839-81df-09720bc9c7d8" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050613}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069253} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069445}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Radius or Diameter of a Circle Given the Circumference"} :step/id #uuid "f9fe7dd8-677c-508f-836a-f00b1739d9cc" :step/math-academy-id 10879 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069551}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069551 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068684 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068545 :question/id #uuid "e18e4cc8-8ecb-53e9-8b12-c9cb5b926aa7" :question/math-academy-id "e-3182"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "89403c66-caac-5f6b-bacd-9f2a74525198" :knowledge-p